\description{
Supervised ordination of samples from any distances: linear discriminant
analysis (\code{\link[=lda]{lda()}}) of their first \eqn{m} principal coordinates
(\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}), with \eqn{m} chosen by leave-one-out classification success,
and a permutation test of the separation of the groups (Anderson &
Willis 2003; "CAP" in PRIMER-PERMANOVA+). Gives the same number of axes,
squared canonical correlations and classification success as
\code{BiodiversityR::CAPdiscrim()}. It is not \code{vegan::capscale()}, which is
distance-based redundancy analysis.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

Canonical analysis of principal coordinates (Anderson & Willis 2003)
finds the axes that best separate known groups of samples in the space
of any distance measure. It computes the principal coordinates of the
distances (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}), and runs linear discriminant analysis (\code{\link[=lda]{lda()}}) on
the first \eqn{m} of them. \code{capda(dm, groups, n_pcoa_axes = m)} gives
the same scores as \code{lda(pcoa(dm, n_axes = m), groups)}.

The canonical correlations \eqn{\delta} are the correlations between
each axis's scores and the groups; \eqn{\delta^2}{delta^2} is the
proportion of the variance of the scores that lies between the groups.
They relate to \code{\link[=lda]{lda()}}'s \code{eig} (\eqn{F}, the ratio of the between- to
the within-group mean squares) by
\eqn{\delta^2 = F (g - 1) / (F (g - 1) + n - g)}{delta^2 = F (g - 1) / (F (g - 1) + n - g)}.

\strong{Choosing m}

Few PCoA axes may miss the differences between the groups; too many fit
noise, and the discriminant analysis needs fewer axes than samples. With
\code{n_pcoa_axes = NULL}, \code{capda()} classifies each sample by the
discriminant analysis of all the other samples (leave-one-out), for each
\eqn{m} from 1 to \code{max_pcoa_axes}, and uses the \eqn{m} that classifies
the most samples correctly; on ties, the smallest. A sample that can't
be classified counts as misclassified: one alone in its group (which,
left out, leaves its group without a mean), and every sample once the
within-group covariance of the other samples is singular. The success
for every \eqn{m} is in the \code{success} attribute, so you can check that
the chosen \eqn{m} is not an isolated peak. The classification uses
each group's proportion of the samples as its prior probability, as
\code{MASS::lda(CV = TRUE)} does by default.

\strong{Permutation test}

The trace statistic, \eqn{\sum \delta^2}{sum(delta^2)}, measures how
well the \eqn{m} PCoA axes separate the groups. The test permutes the
samples' groups (freely, or within \code{strata}) and recomputes the trace on
the same \eqn{m} axes, as Anderson & Willis (2003) do. The choice of
\eqn{m} is not repeated for each permutation. \code{BiodiversityR::CAPdiscrim()}
tests the classification success instead.

\strong{Validate the separation}

Like \code{\link[=lda]{lda()}} and \code{\link[=plsda]{plsda()}}, \code{capda()} is supervised: its plots separate
the groups, if not as well, even when the groups don't differ. The
leave-one-out success and the permutation test are the evidence; the
plot is not.

\strong{Matching BiodiversityR}

\code{BiodiversityR::CAPdiscrim(dm ~ groups, data, m = 0, mmax = 10)} gives
the same \eqn{m}, classification success (its \code{percent} and
\code{percent.level}, as percentages), leave-one-out classes (\code{CV}) and
discriminant scores (\code{x}, up to each axis's sign) as \code{capda(dm, groups, data, max_pcoa_axes = 10)}, and its \code{F} is \code{\link[=lda]{lda()}}'s \code{eig}. Its
\code{manova} holds \code{trace_stat}, as Pillai's trace. It stops with an error
when a group has a single sample; \code{capda()} counts that sample as
misclassified. PRIMER-PERMANOVA+'s CAP scales the axes differently, and
does not use prior probabilities.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::capscale()} ("constrained analysis of principal coordinates")
is distance-based redundancy analysis, ecodive's \code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} and
\code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, not this method. In \code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}, \code{'CAP'} selects
\code{capscale}; use \code{'capda'} for this one. BiodiversityR's version is
\code{CAPdiscrim()}.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An axis's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return mirror
images of the same axis. \code{capda()} flips each axis so that its largest
score in absolute value is positive, and flips its column of \code{scaling}
with it. Results are identical from run to run and for any number of
\code{cpus}; random permutations depend only on \code{seed}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last, the records
of how it was made (see \link{ecodive_steps}): \code{dist}, the distance
matrix's, for the methods that take distances, and \code{ord}, the
ordination's own (its method, display name and settings). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\examples{
    # CAP of iris, from Bray-Curtis distances.
    dm  <- bray(iris[, 1:4])
    ord <- capda(dm, 'Species', iris, permutations = 99)
    plot(ord, 'Species', iris)

    # The number of PCoA axes chosen, and the classification success.
    attr(ord, 'n_pcoa_axes')
    head(attr(ord, 'success'))
    attr(ord, 'loo_table')

    # The squared canonical correlations, and the permutation test.
    attr(ord, 'eig')
    attr(ord, 'p_value')

}
